{
  "id": "nd5levl",
  "title": "Chapter 7: ACIDS, BASES AND SALTS",
  "order": 27,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "o4i918x",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1. Acids"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "pH Scale Range",
          "value": "0 to 14",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Neutral pH",
          "value": "7.0",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Acid Rain pH Threshold",
          "value": "< 5.6",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Bleaching Powder Formula",
          "value": "CaOCl2",
          "icon": "book"
        }
      ],
      "id": "j1pesbb"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "p2dckjn",
      "content": "Acids are substances that produce hydrogen ions (H+) in aqueous solution."
    },
    {
      "id": "fg474dh",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "8yiplvb",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_8yiplvb.png"
    },
    {
      "id": "osysoa6",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "pbbfviy",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_pbbfviy.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kx4gdf8",
      "content": "They are sour in taste and turn blue litmus red."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kpv06sa",
      "content": "They conduct electricity in aqueous solution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u08qzit",
      "content": "Acids react with metals to produce hydrogen gas."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "c85izxg",
      "content": "Acids react with bases to form salt and water (neutralisation)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "67nqv7i",
      "content": "Acids react with carbonates and bicarbonates to produce salt, water and carbon dioxide."
    },
    {
      "id": "lgzkh45",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2. Bases and Alkalis"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "t98p14z",
      "content": "Bases produce hydroxide ions (OH−) in aqueous solution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "m4a03xf",
      "content": "Bases are bitter in taste and soapy to touch."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "js6gkbh",
      "content": "They turn red litmus blue."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kwyr4v1",
      "content": "Alkalis are bases soluble in water (NaOH, KOH)."
    },
    {
      "id": "fvbhsmy",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3. Salts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jww4l1w",
      "content": "Salts are formed by neutralisation reaction between acid and base."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rada7f3",
      "content": "Nature of salt depends on strength of parent acid and base."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "opegfsk",
      "content": "Strong Acid + Strong Base → Neutral Salt."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tsoab2m",
      "content": "Strong Acid + Weak Base → Acidic Salt."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u21u741",
      "content": "Weak Acid + Strong Base → Basic Salt."
    },
    {
      "id": "2u1ps3e",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "4. Strength of Acids and Bases"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3rcfeqa",
      "content": "Strong acids completely ionise in water (HCl, H2SO4, HNO3)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "trn2mcj",
      "content": "Weak acids partially ionise (CH3COOH)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1jxepbd",
      "content": "Strong bases: NaOH, KOH."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vr8kjqr",
      "content": "Weak base: NH4OH."
    },
    {
      "id": "f0k0g1z",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "5. pH Scale"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0galq30",
      "content": "pH scale ranges from 0 to 14."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ie4ftte",
      "content": "pH &lt; 7 : Acidic solution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4geadoz",
      "content": "pH = 7 : Neutral solution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "n14hlqu",
      "content": "pH &gt; 7 : Basic solution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ok2ypb0",
      "content": "Lower pH means stronger acid; higher pH means stronger base."
    },
    {
      "id": "zuyvu2p",
      "type": "numberedList",
      "items": [
        "<strong>Importance of pH in Daily Life</strong>"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "howof5d",
      "content": "pH of stomach acid ≈ 1-2."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "68wx3lx",
      "content": "Tooth decay starts below pH 5.5."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "ACIDS, BASES AND SALTS",
      "id": "yfmtge8"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "185vmh5",
      "content": "Acid rain has pH less than 5.6."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "le9autk",
      "content": "Soil pH affects crop yield."
    },
    {
      "id": "c4d97gb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "7. Water of Crystallisation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ofcsuuj",
      "content": "Water molecules present in salt crystals are called water of crystallisation."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qwqloqe",
      "content": "Example: CuSO4·5H2O (Blue vitriol)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7fhwfrx",
      "content": "Washing soda: Na2CO3·10H2O."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "382fkyk",
      "content": "Plaster of Paris: CaSO4·1/2H2O."
    },
    {
      "id": "s9rqhk1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. Acid–Base Theories"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Acids release H+ (hydronium) ions in aqueous solutions and turn blue litmus paper red.",
        "Bases produce hydroxide (OH-) ions in water, taste bitter, and turn red litmus paper blue.",
        "The pH scale quantifies acidity and alkalinity: pH < 7 is acidic, pH = 7 is neutral, and pH > 7 is basic.",
        "Acid rain occurs when atmospheric pollutants like SO2 and NO2 lower rainwater pH below 5.6.",
        "Water of crystallisation refers to the fixed number of water molecules present in one formula unit of a salt crystal."
      ],
      "id": "b4eau6h"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "duh1gpa",
      "content": "Arrhenius Theory: Acid produces H+ ions, Base produces OH− ions in aqueous solution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zl89k9s",
      "content": "Bronsted-Lowry Theory: Acid donates proton (H+), Base accepts proton."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bh93fac",
      "content": "Lewis Theory: Acid accepts electron pair, Base donates electron pair."
    },
    {
